ByUser:!0,layout:ne,layoutScroll:!0,nodeId:`hgFaN_ZLA`,scopeId:`augiA20Il`,children:h(ks,{accent:`rgb(90, 160, 255)`,ambient:!0,bg:`rgb(243, 236, 227)`,bio:`Hi! I make friendly, well-crafted things for the web.`,builtIn:!0,caption:`Click the computer, shelves, lamp and curtains.`,chatIntro:``,chips:!1,customFont:``,email:`jydnahm@gmail.com`,facts:`Open to work.
Sketches on the train.`,faqs:[{a:`Yes! I'm currently open for freelance projects, collaborations, or entry-level opportunities. Feel free to hit the Contact button if you have something in mind!`,id:`default-item-0`,q:`Are you available for work?`},{a:`I start by understanding the core requirements and mapping out the system architecture. Then I jump into building the UI and backend, test everything thoroughly, and deploy it live.`,id:`default-item-1`,q:`What's your process?`},{a:`It really depends on the complexity. A simple landing page or portfolio usually takes around 1–2 weeks, while a full-stack web or mobile app takes about 3–6 weeks.`,id:`default-item-2`,q:`How long does a project take?`},{a:`I'm based in Yogyakarta, Indonesia (GMT+7), and I'm completely comfortable working remotely with teams or clients from anywhere.`,id:`default-item-3`,q:`Where are you based?`}],files:[{app:`resume`,desc:`A calm daily planner for people who hate planners. I designed the product, the brand and the little animations that make ticking things off feel good.`,folder:`Work`,id:`default-item-0`,kind:`project`,page:1,skills:`Product Design, Figma, Motion`,title:`Pocket Planner`},{app:`resume`,desc:`A design system of 120 components with tokens, docs and a playground. Adopted by three teams in the first month.`,folder:`Work`,id:`default-item-1`,kind:`project`,page:1,skills:`Design Systems, React, Storybook`,title:`Orbit UI Kit`},{app:`resume`,desc:`Identity for an indie lighting studio: logo, packaging and a pixel-art launch site.`,folder:`Work/Branding`,id:`default-item-2`,kind:`project`,page:1,skills:`Branding, Illustration, Framer`,title:`Lumen Brand`},{desc:`Aplikasi layanan pesan-antar makanan berbasis Flutter dengan sistem multi-role, realtime tracking, dan payment gateway.`,folder:`Work/Branding`,id:`default-item-nochy`,kind:`project`,page:1,skills:`Flutter, Dart, Supabase, Midtrans`,title:`Nochy Eat`,image:`/framerusercontent-com/sites/icons/BG-SORE.jpg`},{app:`resume`,desc:`Welcome to my desktop!

- Open the Work folder for projects
- Contact has my links
- Certificates holds my credentials
- Social Media has my links
- Drag windows around, resize them from the corner
- Try the Piano`,folder:``,id:`default-item-3`,kind:`note`,page:1,skills:``,title:`Read me`},{app:`resume`,folder:``,id:`default-item-cv`,kind:`link`,page:1,desc:`ZAIDAN_CV_PRO.PDF`,skills:``,title:`My CV`,link:`/ZAIDAN_CV_PRO.PDF`,image:void 0},{app:`resume`,desc:`This item lives on desktop page 2.
Set a different Page number on any file to move it.`,folder:``,id:`default-item-4`,kind:`note`,page:2,skills:``,title:`Sketchbook`}],floor:`rgb(207, 214, 228)`,frameColor:`rgb(224, 176, 80)`,frameStyle:`gold`,height:`100%`,hints:!0,iconStyle:`color`,id:`hgFaN_ZLA`,intro:!1,introTitle:``,items:[{action:`books`,id:`mJtSmmgTE`,object:`p_shelf`,tags:``,title:``,use:`What this is used for.`},{action:`art`,id:`KBni2Pxae`,object:`p_frame`,tags:``,title:``,use:`What this is used for.`},{action:`about`,id:`dBlPbjLyU`,object:`p_table`,tags:``,title:``,use:`What this is used for.`}],layoutId:`hgFaN_ZLA`,lightButton:!1,mood:`auto`,moodButton:!1,musicButton:!1,musicOnEnter:!1,name:`Zaidan`,osName:`FolioOS`,paintingImage:"/framerusercontent-com/sites/icons/MESSI.png",paintingNote:`Lionel Andreas Messi Cuccutini.`,paintingTitle:`My Idol The GOAT`,palette:`preset`,pet:`cat`,pet2:`none`,pet2Accessory:`none`,pet2Hue:0,pet2Name:``,petAccessory:`none`,petButton:!1,petFollow:!0,petHue:0,petName:``,petScale:2,pixelFont:`pixelify`,playlist:[{title:`The Art of Chasing You`,url:`https://youtu.be/z8J8fOHdpPw?si=dyQLejKAC33Yw47O`},{title:`1 2 3`,url:`https://youtu.be/prte4HRo2ew?si=F0nkmG9ab2qP2CPU`},{title:`bunga`,url:`https://youtu.be/5XXl46ZcXSA?si=80JrhXIg62FBF3YM`}],readingList:[],references:[],bio:`Hello, my name is Zaidan, but you can call me Jay or whatever you prefer. I was born in Banjarnegara, Indonesia, on November 19, 2005. I think my earliest memories of life started when I was around five or six years old, back when I was still in kindergarten. I used to arrive at school early and go home late because both of my parents were teachers and had to be at school by six in the morning. They were always busy, but I knew everything they did was for the future of my younger sibling and me. My family was not wealthy back then, and life was not always easy financially. One memory I still remember was when a friend invited me to play after school. I ended up going to his house without telling my parents. When I finally returned home by pedicab, I saw my mother and father looking extremely worried outside. They had thought I was missing or had even been kidnapped. Well, I was only six years old, so I guess I gave them quite a scare.\n\nAfter kindergarten, I continued my education at an Islamic elementary school. During Grade 1, I remember my father dropping me off at school before going to teach, while I usually went home with our school transport driver, Mr. Hendro. That year was also when I discovered one of my biggest fears: needles. I was terrified of injections, and even now, I still have that fear. I also experienced my first childhood crush on a girl who was kind and beautiful. We were quite close until the school rearranged our classes during the second semester. When I entered Grade 2, something exciting happened. My mother used to tell me that I was a smart kid and might someday skip a grade, and surprisingly, that actually happened. I was placed in a special accelerated class called Grade 2 Pantas. At that age, I felt like the main character in my own story. I thought the world was wonderful and that everything would go according to my wishes. However, that was also when I started noticing economic differences between my family and my classmates. They often talked about travelling to other cities and islands during their holidays, while my holidays were usually spent visiting my grandmother. It was the first time I realized that not everyone lived the same kind of life.\n\nBecause I was in an accelerated program, I skipped Grade 3 and moved directly to Grade 4. I remember having a kind teacher named Mrs. Widi, who taught me mathematics and became my homeroom teacher. One of my favourite memories from that year was a school assignment to build a periscope. While most of my classmates used pipes, I asked my mother to help me make one out of cardboard, and the result was surprisingly beautiful. It was a simple project, but I still remember it because my mother helped me make it. Back then, life still felt wonderful, even though my family\u2019s financial situation was not particularly comfortable. Unfortunately, things started changing when I entered Grade 5. My grades dropped, and I was moved to Class 5A3, separating me from most of my old friends. Some of my classmates bullied me. I was punched, pinched, and had my books and school uniform scribbled on. The strange thing was that I never told my parents about any of it. Whenever they asked how school was going, I always said everything was fine. Maybe I did not want to look weak, or perhaps I simply did not want to make them worry. I eventually decided to study harder because I wanted to leave that situation behind. Thankfully, when I entered Grade 6, I managed to return to Class 6A2 and reunite with my old friends.\n\nGrade 6 was my final year of elementary school. We spent a lot of time preparing for practice examinations and the National Examination. Eventually, the examination day arrived, and I was quite nervous. A few weeks later, all the students gathered in the school hall to receive their results. I do not remember the exact score anymore, but I remember getting around 27 points in total. It was a pretty good result and could have given me a chance to enter one of the most popular junior high schools in my area, SMPN 1 Purbalingga. However, my parents preferred another school, SMP Sambas, so I followed their decision. There is one particular moment I still remember. After the announcement, I went to meet my father to collect my examination results. He congratulated me and told me that he was proud of me. Those simple words meant a lot, especially after everything I had experienced during Grade 5 and Grade 6. That was the end of my elementary school journey.\n\nMy junior high school years were not particularly memorable. I mostly remember spending time with my new friends and developing another crush on a senior girl. However, I was still a shy nerd back then, so all I could do was admire her from a distance. Then came the COVID-19 pandemic. What was supposed to be a two-week school break eventually turned into almost a year of studying from home. There was no National Examination, and our graduation was conducted online. During that period, my daily routine was quite simple. I helped my mother hang the laundry, swept and mopped the floor, washed the dishes, and spent most of my free time watching Korean dramas at night. Interestingly, that period seemed to bring my imagination back to life. I had more time to think about things and imagine the kind of future I wanted to have.\n\nAfter junior high school, I continued my education at a vocational high school, majoring in Computer and Network Engineering. Compared to my previous school years, this was probably one of the most memorable periods of my life. My first year was still affected by pandemic restrictions, so students had to attend school in alternating groups and different schedules. Eventually, we were allowed to attend classes together again, although we still had to wear masks. In Grade 11, I had the opportunity to complete a six-month internship in Purwokerto. At first, I was quite shy and easily discouraged whenever someone spoke harshly to me. Before my official internship schedule started, I had already arrived in Purwokerto and met some students from another school who had been there for three weeks. I eventually became friends with them and stayed in the same rented accommodation. One day, I encountered a technician who suddenly shouted at me in a loud Javanese dialect, asking where I was from. I was extremely annoyed, but I reminded myself that I was there to learn, not to start a fight. During the internship, I travelled to different locations, including Banyumas, Cilacap, Banjarnegara, and Baturraden, helping technicians repair ODP cables, inspect ODC equipment, and handle network problems. I even had the opportunity to visit a Telkom server room. One person I remember particularly well was a technician named Mas Adit. He was friendly, kind, and easygoing. At first, my relationship with the technicians felt rather distant, but after several months, we gradually became closer. When the internship ended, I felt unexpectedly sad about saying goodbye. It reminded me that even the people we meet by chance can eventually become meaningful parts of our lives.\n\nWhen I entered Grade 12, I started seeing life differently. I realized that many of my friends came from families with more difficult financial situations than mine. Some of them had to start working immediately after graduation because they could not afford to continue their education. It made me understand that I had spent too much time looking at people who were better off than me without realizing that there were also people struggling with circumstances much harder than my own. I learned that sometimes we need to look up for motivation, but we also need to look down to appreciate what we already have. I once told my mother that I wanted to work immediately after graduation because I did not want to become a burden to my family. However, she wanted me to continue my education, so I eventually agreed. Unfortunately, getting into university was not as easy as I had imagined. Since I came from a vocational high school, I had only around one month to prepare for the university entrance examinations, covering subjects from three years of general high school. I was selected as an eligible student and chose ITB and UNS as my university preferences, but I did not get accepted through the first selection. I then tried the SNBT examination after studying intensively for a month, but the result was another rejection. I was devastated. I felt mentally exhausted after spending three years studying computers and suddenly having to catch up on years of subjects I had barely studied. Eventually, I told my mother that I still wanted to go to university, but I no longer wanted to take another entrance examination. With my family\u2019s support, I continued my education at Universitas Ahmad Dahlan.\n\nMy first five semesters at UAD went relatively smoothly. However, during my sixth semester, I faced one of the most stressful experiences of my university life while taking Research Methodology. I had to attend several supervision sessions with my lecturer, who was actually very knowledgeable and gave me excellent research guidance. However, communicating with her and getting her approval was sometimes difficult. Throughout the supervision process, I only met her in person around seven times, while the rest of our meetings were conducted online. I tried my best to follow every instruction and complete all the revisions, even though sometimes I felt frustrated when I saw other students getting approval more easily. Eventually, I passed my proposal seminar, and the examiners gave me positive feedback, allowing me to proceed to the thesis stage. However, my supervisor\u2019s grade had not been submitted. I contacted her politely to ask about it, but my message was only left on read. A few days later, I checked the academic system and discovered that I had received a D. I was shocked because the examiners had already approved my proposal. After trying to contact several lecturers, I eventually learned that my supervisor\u2019s father had passed away. I understood the situation, but at that moment, I was emotionally exhausted and frustrated because I had been waiting for an answer for so long.\n\nI remember sitting quietly at my grandmother\u2019s dining table during a family wedding when my mother noticed that something was wrong. I told her everything, and my father listened as well. My mother tried to reassure me that every problem had a solution, while my father told me that perhaps this was God\u2019s way of teaching me to become more mature. I honestly wanted to cry. I tried covering my face with my hands, but the tears still came out. The following day, I returned to Yogyakarta and asked several lecturers for help. Thankfully, one of them, Mr. Fajri, helped me resolve the issue. The grade was eventually corrected, and I received an A. That experience taught me that sometimes a situation that seems impossible still has a way out, as long as we keep trying and do not stop asking for help.\n\nNow, I am in my seventh semester, and I feel that my social circle has become much smaller. I probably only have around four close friends left, but they are people I have known since my second semester. As I get closer to graduation, I am beginning to understand what people mean when they say that the working world is not as easy as it seems. I used to dream of working for major technology companies such as Gojek, Shopee, Google, or Amazon. However, the closer I get to entering the workforce, the more complicated everything seems. I once received an internship opportunity at a relatively large company, but the position was unrelated to IT, the location was about an hour away from where I lived, and it was unpaid. I did not mind working without a salary if the experience was worthwhile, but considering the distance and the nature of the position, I eventually decided not to take it. I also applied to many other internship opportunities, but most of them ended in rejection. Sometimes I wonder whether the projects I have built and the skills I have developed are still not enough to convince a company to give me a chance. I have also started noticing news about experienced programmers losing their jobs, declining salaries, and the impact of artificial intelligence on the technology industry. All of this has made me rethink my expectations. Perhaps I have been dreaming too much without fully understanding how difficult the real world can be. Nowadays, I want to focus more on improving my English skills and exploring opportunities to work abroad, even if the job I eventually get is not entirely related to my field of study. I want to see more of the world and discover opportunities that I might not find here.\n\nLooking back, I realize that the biggest thing that has changed throughout my life is the way I see the world. When I was a little kid, I thought everything was wonderful and that life would always go according to my wishes. But as I grew older, I experienced economic differences, bullying, failure, separation, disappointment, and many other things I had never imagined before. I once felt special when I entered an advanced class, powerless when I was bullied, proud when my father congratulated me, and sad when I had to say goodbye to the people I met during my internship. I also experienced the disappointment of failing university entrance examinations and the exhaustion of dealing with academic problems. But despite everything, I am still here. I am still trying, still learning, and still figuring out what kind of person I want to become.\n\nThere are two song lyrics that I always remember whenever life gets difficult. The first one comes from a song I used to listen to when I was in Grade 1: \u201cWhen your beautiful dreams never come true, well, just let it be.\u201d The second one comes from a song I used to listen to during my vocational school internship: \u201cIf I made it through yesterday, then I can face tomorrow too.\u201d Those two lines may sound simple, but they represent my journey so far. I do not know what tomorrow will bring, and I do not know whether all my dreams will eventually come true. But at least I have survived many things I once thought I would never be able to overcome.\n\nI am Zaidan, although some people know me as Jay, and this is my story. Not the story of someone who always gets what he wants, but the story of someone who keeps moving forward, even after repeatedly discovering that the world does not always work the way he hopes it will.\n\nTo be continued...`,facts:``,resumeText:`# Experience
Fullstack Developer | NOCHY E-Commerce Platform
- Built a Next.js & TypeScript apparel store with Midtrans payment and Supabase database.
Mobile Developer | NOCHY EAT (On-Demand Food Delivery)
- Built a 2D action platformer game using Go (Golang) and Ebitengine compiled to WebAssembly.
Fullstack Web Developer | Muntjul Group Corporate Profile
- Developed an industrial corporate profile website using Laravel, MySQL, and 3D vector graphics.
# Education
Computer Science | Universitas Ahmad Dahlan · 2023 - Now
Computer and Network | SMK N 1 Purbalingga · 2020 - 2023
Junior High School | SMP Istiqomah Sambas · 2017 - 2019
Elementary School | MI Istiqomah Sambas · 2012 - 2016
# Skills
Next.js, React, TypeScript, JavaScript, Go (Golang), PHP, Laravel, Flutter, Dart, Python, Java, Kotlin, Tailwind CSS, Bootstrap, Ebitengine, Node.js, PostgreSQL, Supabase, MySQL, Firebase, REST API, Git, Vercel, Midtrans API`,role:`Software Enginer, Product Manager, Quality Assurance`,sections:!0,socials:[{id:`default-item-0`,label:`GitHub`},{id:`default-item-1`,label:`LinkedIn`},{id:`default-item-2`,label:`Instagram`}],sound:!1,status:`Open to work`,style:{height:`100%`,width:`100%`},trinkets:[{icon:`key`,id:`default-item-0`,name:`House keys`,purpose:`Opens the front door. They live in this dish so they are never lost. (They are always lost.)`},{icon:`carkey`,id:`default-item-1`,name:`Car key`,purpose:`Starts the little blue hatchback. Beeps twice if I forget where I parked it.`},{icon:`coin`,id:`default-item-2`,name:`Lucky coin`,purpose:`Found on a sidewalk in 2019. Flipped before every big decision. Heads means ship it.`},{icon:`watch`,id:`default-item-3`,name:`Pocket watch`,purpose:`Runs exactly five minutes fast, so I am never late to anything.`},{icon:`keychain`,id:`default-item-4`,name:`Pixel charm`,purpose:`A tiny heart from my first game jam. It rides in every pocket for luck.`},{icon:`pin`,id:`default-item-5`,name:`Enamel pin`,purpose:`One pin for every project that shipped. This one was a good week.`}],wall:`rgb(201, 195, 189)`,weather:`clear`,width:`100%`,wood:`rgb(168, 104, 60)`})})})}),h(`div`,{id:`overlay`})]})})}),[`.framer-tSpxR.framer-lux5qc, .framer-tSpxR .framer-lux5qc { display: block; }`,`.framer-tSpxR.framer-72rtr7 { background-color: #d6ccc2; height: 1000px; overflow: var(--overflow-clip-fallback, clip); position: relative; width: 1200px; }`,`.framer-tSpxR .framer-1fx4p2d-container { flex: none; height: 100vh; left: calc(50.00000000000002% - 80% / 2); position: fixed; top: calc(50.00000000000002% - 100vh / 2); width: 80%; z-index: 1; }`,`@media (min-width: 810px) and (max-width: 1199.98px) { .framer-tSpxR.framer-72rtr7 { width: 810px; }}`,`@m